/*! \page developer_build_dependency_page Dependency Files

\ref developer_build_source_page   (Prev) &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
\ref developer_build_pattern_page (Next)
<BR>

The pattern rules used by PSCF to compile a source file create both an
object file with file name suffix .o or .ou and a corresponding dependency
file with file suffix .d and .du.  The rule to compile a standard C++ 
files with filename suffix .cpp creates an object file with suffix .o
and a dependency file with suffix .d. The corresponding rule to compile
a CUDA source file with suffix .cu creates an object file with suffix
.ou and a dependency file with .du. 

Each dependency file contains a definition of a makefile rule that lists
all of the files that are prerequisites of an associated object file, but 
that has no recipe. The pre-requisites of an object file include both the
the associated \*.cpp or \*.cu source file that is compiled to create it
and all of the \*.h header files and (in some cases) \*.tpp template 
implementation files that are directly or indirectly included into the 
source file via preprocessor \#include directives.
The dependency file associated with an object file named Class.o thus 
contains a makefile rule that, is simplified form, might look something 
like this: 
\code

Class.o: Class.cpp Class.h Header1.h Header2.h ....

\endcode
Here, Header1.h, Header2.h, etc. represent header files other than the
header containing the class definition that are directly or indirectly
included into Class.cpp.

A rule defined in a dependency file never contains a recipe. The recipe
required to construct the associated object is instead inferred from an 
applicable pattern rule.  Pattern rules for compiling source files with
suffix .cpp and/or .cu files are defined in a pattern rule file named
patterns.mk in the enclosing namespace level directory.

Though not shown in the above example, the paths to the target and
prerequisites files are all actually given in all dependency files as 
absolute paths, i.e., as complete paths that show the location 
relative to root of the entire filesystem.  As a result, the paths in 
real dependency files are generally much longer than those shown in 
the above simplified example.

The list of prerequisites generated by the PSCF build system always
includes the config.mk files located in the relevant build directory
(src or bld), in addition to all source code file prerequisites. The
configuration file is included as a prerequisite because this file
defines makefile variables that control conditional compilation of
some features.  Changes in a relevant config.mk file can thus change
the content of a translation unit that is passed to the compiler by
the precompiler, and can thus change the content of the associated
object file target.

PSCF makefiles in the src/ and bld/ directories contain include 
directives that include the dependency files for all object files that 
the makefile is responsible for creating. This is shown more explicitly 
in the discussion of \ref developer_build_makefile_page "makefiles". 
As a result, make is generally able to find a rule with a complete 
prerequisite list for every existing object file target it may need 
to update.

The list of prerequisites given in a dependency file is generated
automatically by a script that calls the gcc compiler with an option
that instructs the compiler to analyze C++ "#include" directives.
This compiler option was specifically designed to allow the GNU 
g++ compiler to be used to automatically generate prerequisite lists.

<BR>
